<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # How Do I Find a Book in the Library? – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> In the Library</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Do I Find a Book in the Library?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Rewrite the end of a story.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write descriptive reviews of books read in the library by providing a summary of the content and their opinion (e.g., 'This book is about a boy who discovers a hidden treasure. It is funny and exciting.'). Can rewrite story endings based on library events (e.g., 'And then, they found a treasure in the library.'). Can add new ideas to simple stories related to library themes (e.g., 'Then, the dog found a hidden book.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different library sections (fiction, non-fiction) and types of books (mystery, adventure, funny). Students quickly brainstorm words related to 'library' and 'stories' on the board or in their notebooks. For example, 'book', 'read', 'find', 'interesting', 'librarian'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to describe a book using past simple and adjectives, and how to start rewriting a story ending. For example, "Last week, I read an *interesting* *book* about a *boy* who *found* a *secret chapter*. The story *was* very *exciting*." Then, "To rewrite the end, I can start with 'And then, he...' or 'Suddenly,...'". The teacher emphasizes short and long vowel sounds in words like /ɪ/ in 'pick' (fiction) and /aɪ/ in 'find'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we write about the beginning or the end of a story today?" (Expected answer: "The end!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a book is 'interesting', do you want to read it?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "In the Library")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt (1-2 paragraphs) about a character in a library finding a mysterious book, with an open or incomplete ending. This text will be displayed on the board or as a handout.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Story: "Lily *went* to the *library* to *find* an *interesting book*. She *searched* the *fiction section* and *found* a shiny blue *book* with a mysterious *title*. When she *opened* the *first chapter*, she *read* about a *librarian* who *found* a hidden *map* inside a very *old book*. Lily *was surprised* and *wanted* to *find* out what happened next, but the *last page* was missing!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writing: Can comprehend short narratives about library experiences to prepare for rewriting story endings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who went to the library?" (Gist question)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did Lily find?" (Information matching)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was the last page there? True or False?" (True/False)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practice using past simple tense for story descriptions, modals 'can/should' for suggestions, and imperative verbs, integrating vocabulary related to the library scenario (book, library, catalog, author, chapter, summary, librarian, read, find, interesting, quiet, helpful).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences from the story, filling in past simple verbs or library nouns.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">   Example: "Lily ____ (go) to the ____ (library)." "She ____ (find) an ____ (interesting) ____ (book)." "The *librarian* ____ (help) her *find* the *book*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice (Guided Rewriting):</b> The teacher provides sentence frames and prompts for students to generate ideas for a new ending.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">   Example prompts: "And then, Lily ____ (verb in past tense) a new ____ (noun)." "This book ____ (was/was not) ____ (adjective)." "What *should* Lily do next?" "She *can* look in the *catalog* for more *resources*." (Students choose verbs/nouns/adjectives and write in full sentences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Peer Checking):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes two new sentences for Lily's story ending. They exchange sentences and check if their partner used past tense verbs and any library words. They also listen for correct intonation when asking questions like "Can you read your new sentence?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "In the Library")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product – "Rewrite My Story's Ending!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Students receive a worksheet with the incomplete story from Stage 2.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. They rewrite the ending of Lily's story in 3-5 sentences, adding new ideas or events that could happen "In the Library." They must use past simple tense.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. After writing the ending, they write one sentence giving their opinion about their new ending using an adjective (e.g., 'This ending is *surprising* and *exciting*.').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Students are encouraged to use at least 4-5 vocabulary words from the list (e.g., *chapter*, *librarian*, *shelf*, *resource*, *find*, *read*, *interesting*, *helpful*, *many*). They are reminded that they *should* be *careful* with their writing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist while students write and then collects written products for quick review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their rewritten story ending and their one-sentence opinion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses past simple tense to describe actions (e.g., *found*, *read*, *went*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes at least 3-4 target library vocabulary words (e.g., *book*, *library*, *librarian*, *chapter*, *find*, *read*, *interesting*, *shelf*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The rewritten ending is coherent and adds new ideas related to the library theme.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes a simple opinion sentence about the new ending.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two quick prompts before leaving.</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. I can write a new ending for a story using words about the library. (Circle one: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. I used past tense verbs correctly in my writing. (Circle one: Yes / A little / No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was easy about rewriting the story ending today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What new words or grammar did I use most in my writing today?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the lesson helped students rewrite story endings effectively? The guided practice with sentence frames seemed to build their confidence.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which grammar points (past simple, modals) need more practice in writing for this grade level? Some students still struggle with irregular past simple verbs; a short review for that might be needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I better scaffold the addition of 'new ideas' in story rewriting for future lessons? Providing a small bank of possible "library events" (e.g., "found a secret passage," "met a talking *book*") could spark more creativity.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> "In our next and final lesson for this theme, we will use our rewritten story endings and reviews to create a library display. We will summarize our stories for others to read and integrate all our skills to present our favorite new stories to the class as a 21st Century Skills Project."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>